<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fulda Abbey</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/744">744</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fulda">Fulda</a> Abbey was a Benedictine monastery in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, founded in 744 by Sturm, a disciple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-boniface">Saint Boniface</a>, who was buried there in 754. Under abbots such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hrabanus-maurus">Rabanus Maurus</a> it became one of the leading centres of learning in the Carolingian empire. Its abbots became princes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, and in 1752 the abbey was raised to a prince-bishopric.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fulda-abbey.jpg" alt="Fulda Abbey" width="320" height="192" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Laurens · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Black_St_George%27s_Cross.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
